WEBVTT

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:04.600
So module one left us with the four quality controlled donor objects.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:09.160
So now module two puts them together and asks the first real biological question of the course.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:13.900
The question is narrower than it sounds and the whole module turns on keeping it narrow.

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:18.040
So when you compare two donors and a cell type looks different,

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.600
there are two completely different things that could be true.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:27.240
There might be more of those cells in one donor or the cells themselves might be expressing different genes.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.140
Those are not the same findings,

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.660
and they do not have the same causes.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:35.660
So telling them apart is the objective here.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.460
So this is the longest module.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.560
It'll take approximately 45 minutes,

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:43.060
and it has the same structure as module one.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.840
So overview before you begin,

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.980
and then the methods results interpretation and the appendix.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:54.340
So methods has seven steps,

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:57.120
and each of the table here at the top

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.500
is going to map each one to the notebook section.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.500
Two of those pairing are worth flagging now.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:06.120
So just so you're aware that steps one and two

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.480
both live in notebook section one,

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.220
and steps six and seven both live in section five,

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.340
because each case is a single call,

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:17.520
and they do both.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.500
So then step three is a harmony,

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:23.840
and it is a step that people most often misread.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.600
So really donor is a technical factor here.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:29.940
I'm going to go down to step three.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.160
Yeah, there we go.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:50.000
So step three is a harmony step where donor really

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.700
is a technical factor in the multi donor atlas.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.180
So harmony adjusts the embedding so that the neighborhoods

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.100
mixed donors while local structure survives.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.840
And then this takes us into step four,

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.900
where the diagnostics that will tell us

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.680
whether this actually worked.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.240
So then we go into the specific results

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.860
and you can see that the mean foreign donor

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.520
nearest neighbor fraction rises from 0.394 beforehand

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:28.640
to approximately 0.685 after at K of 30.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:34.480
So this means that the harmony actually

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:35.960
did work in the donors mix.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.100
So that's the claim.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:39.660
And then immediately, the honest qualifier,

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:42.740
because this is the habit that the course is trying

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:46.180
to really build, that number is a global average.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:48.520
So per compartment, agreement is not

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:52.180
unanimous donor silhouettes fall after harmony in three

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:55.480
departments and move the other way in immune, which is the smallest

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:57.500
and was already been mixed.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:02:59.480
So the instruction in the document

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.840
is to quote the exception rather than average it

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:04.160
into the global number.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:08.280
One additional thing harmony does not do,

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:12.900
and this catches people, is it writes corrected embeddings.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.140
So it does not touch the expression matrix.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:19.560
neighbors and clustering changes, differential expression still runs on

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:22.520
uncorrected expression with donor handled in the model.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:31.220
So then the cell type classes, so concatenation keeps the 18,884

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:39.440
nuclei and the four compartments across the 6,928 epithelios,

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:44.000
6,205 endothelial, 3,190 stromal.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.400
and 2,556 immune.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:52.900
So the fifth group of the five nuclei is unlabeled

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.860
and it falls below the minimal group size

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.740
and it is dropped and reported rather than quietly merged

00:03:57.880 --> 00:03:58.520
into the neighbors.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.660
So this is, you know, we're able to look at a before

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:06.280
and after of a UMAP representation

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:08.600
so that you can kind of see, you know,

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:11.359
some of the different heterogeneity between the donors

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:16.340
and how Harmony actually is able to properly apply batch

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:16.799
correction.

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:21.680
So now as we transition to the specific notebook files,

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:26.220
so now you'll see it similarly that module two mirrors

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.860
the PDF supporting documents.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:30.960
So there's going to be six numbers.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:34.300
Section one loads the module one objects

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:36.360
and computes the joint embedding.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.580
And then after, of course, so we do a setup,

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:40.820
and then we do concatenate.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:44.740
And then section two is going to be the harmony

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:46.380
and then scores the actual mixing.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:51.180
And then section three is where the modeling choice gets

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:51.960
made explicit.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.400
So we collapse the azimuthal fine labels

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:55.820
into four course classes.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:01.140
And the original-- so then the original five department

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:03.180
versions had--

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:03.800
so a fifth group.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.480
But we just collapsed it to the four for this training

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:09.000
objective.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:14.900
And then the fourth section is the marker heat map

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:16.620
in the composition weighted pseudobalk.

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:19.860
So the second product is worth naming

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:22.000
because module four consumes it.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.100
So pseudobalk means summing the single nucleus

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:28.160
counts back up to one profile per donor

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:32.560
so that a single cell data set can be set beside a bulk ones.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:35.540
Waiting it by composition rather than taking a maximum

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:37.159
across the labels matters

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:41.280
because the weighted version tracks GTX long noticeably

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:45.220
better, and module four shows that in side by side

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:46.940
when we execute module four.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:50.420
So then module five does a differential expression,

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:52.000
and then pathway enrichment.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:56.520
So enrichment runs against the measure gene universe

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:58.500
and not the full library background.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:01.920
If you use the whole library, your cutting genes,

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:04.020
the same never measured is available,

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:06.160
but absent and your significance inflates.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:08.920
So the effect is worse on a short gene list,

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:13.680
which is exactly when people reach four in Richmond.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:17.760
So this brings us to then the sixth section,

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:20.460
which closes the loop back to the opening question

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:23.320
of the composition against expression side by side,

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:25.560
so you can see which one is carrying the difference.

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:30.240
So next we will integrate and look at module three.

